  <prompt>Run the closed secondary hit-site sweep from design §5 and record a per-item verdict (n/a or fixed) for each: H6 (`ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler` primary-handler cast + note the named-client header/query log redaction), `System.Linq.Async` refs (CS0121 — `AsyncEnumerable` moved inbox), `\bfield\b` identifiers inside property accessors (C# 14 contextual keyword), `IPNetwork`/`ForwardedHeadersOptions.KnownNetworks` → `KnownIPNetworks`, `IExceptionHandler.TryHandleAsync` returning true now suppressing error-log + diagnostics metrics, configuration `null`-preservation (nulls no longer coerced to "", empty arrays bind empty), `DOTNET_*` App Service settings now overriding runtimeconfig, and `MailAddress` now rejecting consecutive dots. Fix where the grep hits; mark n/a where it does not; no behavior regression.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in ASP.NET Core forwarded headers, HttpClientFactory, exception handling, configuration binding, and C# 14 language changes. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>
  <goal>A document `notes/secondary-sweep.md` with each of the ~8 items, its grep result, verdict (n/a / fixed), and — where fixed — the file+change; BFF builds green; no behavior regression in the affected paths.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>design §5 "Secondary audits". Research greps found NO `ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler` in `src/server` → H6 likely n/a (re-verify after any Dataverse-HTTP-unification merge). Each item is a small, bounded check: either the pattern exists (fix per design §5) or it does not (record n/a with the grep). The `IExceptionHandler` item matters because dashboards/alerts may key on suppressed diagnostics — note `ExceptionHandlerOptions.SuppressDiagnosticsCallback` as the opt-out if relevant. The config-null and `DOTNET_*` items require auditing `appsettings*.json` + App Service settings, not just code.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/** (wherever a grep item hits)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/appsettings.json</file>
      <file role="new">projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/notes/secondary-sweep.md</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="005" status="pending">BFF builds on net10.</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">The checklist is a CLOSED SET (all ~8 items) — each MUST have a grep result + verdict; a partial sweep is a defect (spec FR-10).</constraint>
    <constraint source="NFR-01">Any fix must be behavior-preserving (e.g. `KnownIPNetworks` migration must keep the same trusted-network config; config-null handling must not change what code reads).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">For the `IExceptionHandler` diagnostics-suppression item: if any dashboard/alert keys on those metrics, apply `SuppressDiagnosticsCallback` opt-out; otherwise record the log-redaction note for future debuggers.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>.NET 9/10 ASP.NET Core + C# 14 secondary breaking changes</topic>
    <files>
      <file>projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/design.md</file>
      <file>projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/notes/breaking-changes-rescrape.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="secondary-audits" location="projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/design.md">design §5 secondary audits — the exact grep list + per-item fix guidance.</pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Load spec FR-10 + design §5 secondary audits + the task-001 re-scrape note. Declare RIGOR: FULL.</step>
    <step order="1">For each of the ~8 items, run the grep; record the result.</step>
    <step order="2">Where the pattern exists, apply the design §5 fix (behavior-preserving); where absent, record n/a with the grep evidence.</step>
    <step order="3">Audit `appsettings*.json` for literal nulls / empty arrays (config-null-preservation) and App Service `DOTNET_*` settings precedence (coordinate the App Service audit with task 050's evidence gathering).</step>
    <step order="4">Build BFF green; write `notes/secondary-sweep.md` (closed checklist + verdicts).</step>
    <step order="5">Update `tasks/TASK-INDEX.md`: set 013 status to ✅.</step>
    <step order="6">Document any deviation in `projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/notes/`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If any secondary item turns out to be behavior-affecting in a production path (e.g. `MailAddress` consecutive-dot rejection breaks a real address the email-intelligence path handles), STOP and surface per CLAUDE.md §6.5 — it may need its own remediation task rather than an in-line tweak.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <tools>
    <tool name="dotnet">Build BFF</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/** (fixes where grep hits)</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/dotnet-10-upgrade-r1/notes/secondary-sweep.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">`notes/secondary-sweep.md` covers ALL ~8 secondary items, each with a grep result and an explicit n/a-or-fixed verdict.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every "fixed" item is behavior-preserving and the BFF builds green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The `IExceptionHandler` diagnostics-suppression + named-client log-redaction behaviors are explicitly assessed (fixed or documented).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: no item is left unassessed (no illustrative subset).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>spec FR-10. H6 is expected n/a per research greps — re-verify. Coordinate the App Service `DOTNET_*`/config audit with task 050.

  COMPLETION (2026-08-12): Closed 10-item sweep (8 from design §5/FR-10 + 2 from task-001 re-scrape follow-up #2). **ALL 10 items = n/a → ZERO code changes.** BFF net10 Release build GREEN (0 errors, 21 warnings = unchanged post-012 baseline; **0× CS9258/CS9259** = definitive `field`-keyword clearance). Verdicts: (1) H6 `ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler` 0 matches; (2) `System.Linq.Async` 0 csproj refs; (3) `field` keyword — only comments/strings + method-body locals, no property-accessor identifier, build 0× CS9258; (4) `IPNetwork`/`KnownNetworks` 0 matches; (5) `IExceptionHandler.TryHandleAsync` — no interface impl, only lambda `UseExceptionHandler` with explicit `logger.LogError` (MiddlewarePipelineExtensions.cs:29-60), suppression change n/a, `SuppressDiagnosticsCallback` not needed; (6) config-null — 2 nulls both in `appsettings.template.json` (CopyToPublishDirectory=Never, not runtime-loaded) + all consumers `IsNullOrWhiteSpace`/`??` null-tolerant; (7) `DOTNET_*` — 0 App Service app settings in infra (`.github` hits are CI runner vars + `DOTNET_VERSION:'8.x'`=task 040); (8) `MailAddress` — 2 outbound-recipient validators only, no inbound parsing, escalation trigger did NOT fire; (9) C#14 span/array overload — build clean, no ambiguity; (10) `XmlSerializer` 0 matches (re-scrape's ComposeService note stale). Escalation triggers (MailAddress, config) did not fire. Step 9.5 quality gates N/A (zero source/test changes — doc-only, same as task 010). Downstream coordination noted: task 050 (live-env `DOTNET_*` confirm), task 040 (CI `DOTNET_VERSION`), task 031/032 (2× residual NU1510 on BFF — H4/P0 hygiene, not a behavioral sweep item), task 051 (ActivitySource/W3C propagator smoke). Full closed checklist: `notes/secondary-sweep.md`.</notes>

